      "title": "12.2.1 Basicity of ammonia",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "b07b1f6c-f5a4-509d-ae86-73be5bffb0d4",
          "pdf_from": 274,
          "pdf_to": 275,
          "note": "Topic 12.2.1, printed pages 274-275."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Ammonia behaves as a Lowry-Brønsted base by accepting a proton (H⁺) from an acid to form ammonium:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{NH3_{(aq)} + H^{+1}_{(aq)} <=> NH4^{1+}_{(aq)}}",
            "text_alt": "NH₃(aq) + H⁺¹(aq) ⇌ NH₄¹⁺(aq)"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "It dissolves in water to form ammonium hydroxide (NH₄OH) and equilibrium is established between ammonia molecules and ammonium ions in the solution."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{NH3_{(aq)} + H2O_{(l)} <=> NH4^{1+}_{(aq)} + OH^{1-}_{(aq)}}\\qquad K_b = 1.8\\times10^{-5}",
            "text_alt": "NH₃(aq) + H₂O(l) ⇌ NH₄¹⁺(aq) + OH¹⁻(aq)   Kb = 1.8×10⁻⁵"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Ammonia solution is a weak base due to the low basicity constant (Kb) and the equilibrium position being towards the far left side."
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
